<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yosef, Yusuf</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph is a figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a>, the first of the two sons of Jacob and Rachel and the founder of the Tribe of Joseph among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>. According to Genesis, his envious brothers sold him into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where he was imprisoned until he correctly interpreted the dreams of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a>, rose to second-in-command and saved Egypt during a famine. Jacob's family then settled in the Land of Goshen, so that his story explains Israel's residence in Egypt. Scholars differ on its historical background and on the date and development of its composition. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, as Yusuf, he is regarded as a prophet, and Christian tradition often reads him as a precursor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>.</p></div>
